# Secrets never enter the build context, and never an image layer.
#
# `COPY frontend/ ./` in stage 1 is unguarded, so any .env a Vite dev creates
# would be baked into a builder LAYER -- which outlives the build context and is
# far harder to notice or undo than a stray file on disk. frontend/.gitignore
# ignores .env and .env.* ; .dockerignore did not. Nothing leaks today, no .env
# exists, and this closes the path before one does.
#
# `.env.*` matters more than `.env`: Vite's actual convention is .env.local,
# .env.development and .env.production, so the bare filename is the LEAST likely
# one to appear.
#
# The two re-inclusions mirror frontend/.gitignore:19-20, which deliberately
# un-ignores .env.example and .env.test because they are meant to be readable.
# This is not hypothetical: ./.env.example is a tracked file today, so a bare
# `**/.env.*` would have excluded committed documentation -- a smaller bug in the
# opposite direction.
#
# Order is load-bearing: .dockerignore resolves by LAST matching pattern, so the
# negations must follow the broad excludes. Verified by seeding all six names on
# disk and reading them back out of a real image layer -- not by analogy to
# gitignore, whose `!` semantics differ.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
!**/.env.test
